      "worked": "At $x=-2$:\n$$\\begin{align*}f(-2)&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\\\f_L(-2)&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\\\f_R(-2)&=-2a\\end{align*}$$\nFor continuity, all three values must agree:\n$$\\begin{align*}-2a&=-\\frac{1}{2}\\\\a&=\\frac{1}{4}\\end{align*}$$\nAt $x=0$:\n$$\\begin{align*}f(0)&=0\\\\f_L(0)&=0\\\\f_R(0)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThese three values agree for every $a$. The reciprocal branch is restricted to $x\\le -2$, so its denominator is never zero there. Each branch is continuous on its interval. Therefore $f$ is continuous at all points in its domain when $a=\\frac{1}{4}$.",
